 \requestedchange{1} Correct the description of SEDAR's comment-before-mutation and restore-after-mutation mechanism. Clearly explain the actual methodological differences between MORPH and SEDAR, avoiding misleading characterizations of the latter.

-\reply TODO.
+\reply
+We apologize for the inaccurate description. The original paper described \sedar{}'s mutability refinement as ``destructive sanitization,'' which was incorrect: \sedar{} temporarily comments out parser-incompatible fragments during mutation and restores them before sending the mutated SQL to the target DBMS, so it does not permanently remove query logic. We have revised the paper as follows:
+(1) We have corrected the description of \sedar{} throughout the paper. In Section~II, we have described its mutability refinement including the restoration step, revised the motivating example to show the commented fragment as an intermediate state that is restored before execution, and changed \sedar{}'s \textit{Cross-stmt.\ Deps.} entry in Table~I to \partmark{}. In the Introduction, the old version's sentence ``generated test cases are often sanitized by removing or commenting out unsupported constructs, which may simplify complex SQL structures and weaken execution behaviors'' is replaced with ``parser-incompatible fragments are handled after translation rather than resolved during it. For example, \sedar{} temporarily comments them out during mutation and restores them before execution on the target DBMS, which retains the original query logic but leaves translation errors in these fragments unrepaired.''
+
+(2) We have clarified the methodological differences in Sections~II and~VI. \sedar{} translates each statement of a test case individually under its referenced source-side sub-schema, whereas \tool{} propagates target-side schema and type context across statements via the AQT. Moreover, \tool{} repairs incompatibilities with parser and execution feedback before mutation, whereas \sedar{}'s restoration retains the hidden fragments without repairing translation errors in them. Neither strategy guarantees semantic equivalence of the mutated queries.
+(3) We have removed the statements attributing \tool{}'s improvements to permanent loss of query logic in \sedar{} (Sections~I, IV-C, IV-D, and VI), and added a controlled comparison in Section~IV-C. For each of the four target DBMSs, we applied both mutation-handling strategies to the same 300 \sedar{}-translated seeds and ran \griffin{} for 24 hours with five repetitions. \tool{}'s handling achieves \TODO{a}\%, \TODO{b}\%, \TODO{c}\%, and \TODO{d}\% higher branch coverage on DuckDB, MonetDB, Virtuoso, and ClickHouse, respectively.

 \requestedchange{2} Moderate the novelty claims and better distinguish MORPH from existing SQL translation approaches, particularly SEDAR, RISE, QTRAN, and CrackSQL. Explain more concretely what AQT and its associated mechanisms contribute beyond existing techniques, especially in the context of DBMS fuzzing.

